h the  pharmacy or the laboratory  with patient care so let me look at each  of these points and  provide a little bit more data to  support these comments  we'll start off with the impact of  empiric therapy  and i'm going to cite four studies and  these in fact are  four reviews looking at a large number  of studies of sepsis in patients  so as an example the first the first  study by paul  and co-workers looked at 70 studies of  sepsis in adults  and what they found was that in more  than half of these studies  greater than 25 percent of the patients  received empiric therapy that was  inappropriate  raymond and co-workers did the same type  of study but in this case they looked at  septic patients  with gra with infections caused by  gram-negative bacilli  and of the 57 studies that they looked  at  almost three-quarters of those studies  again  saw that greater than 25 of the patients  were treated  inappropriately with empiric therapy  herrera and co-workers reviewed 191  studies  of almost 75 000 patients  and they found that in one-third of the  patients  the empiric therapy was inappropriate  and finally silverberg and co-workers in  a multi-center study of 175 u.s  hospitals  observed that when inappropriate therapy  was administered  the patients with infections caused by  carbon and resistant organisms  the mortality was four-fold greater than  what you saw with  susceptible patients and that's logical  because these patients  have infections with organisms that are  resistant to almost all  antibiotics so you would expect that  you're going to see a higher proportion  of these patients  being treated inappropriately so this  underscores the value of diagnostics for  these patients  because the diagnostic tests are going  to support the detection  and diagnosis of drug resistant  infections  it's going to reduce the use of  inappropriate antibiotics because it's  going to be guiding  the therapy with the antimicrobial  susceptibility test  this results in improved patient  outcomes  and because the patients are in the  hospital for a shorter period of time  because they're doing fewer diagnostic  tests because they have a diagnosis  this is going to decrease the hospital  cost  blood cultures are the key diagnostic  test for substance management  because it's going to identify the  organism responsible for sepsis  it's going to allow us to identify those  organisms and perform antimicrobial  susceptibility tests  but then we can select the most  appropriate antibiotic  for the treatment of these patients but  for this to be valuable  the blood culture results have to be  rapid and to have to be accurate  so let's talk about each of these issues  for rapid accurate suscep blood  cultures requires the use of best  practices for both the collection of the  blood cultures  and the processing of the blood cultures  what this  means is that we have to disinfect the  skin properly so we reduce  contaminated blood cultures we have to  collect the sufficient volume of blood  to maximize the recovery of the pathogen  that is to maximize the proportion of  collective blood cultures that are going  to be  positive and we have to implement good  workflow practices  in both the transport of the specimen to  the laboratory  as well as processing that specimen in  the laboratory  so again let's look at these issues  we'll start off with contaminated blood  cultures  what's been found with a contaminated  blood culture is that these are  associated with a significant  increase in hospital costs there's an  increase in the pharmacy cost  because antibiotics are being  administered unnecessarily  and there's an increase in the  laboratory cost  because you have to verify that in fact  that  isolate which turns out to be a  contaminant was truly in that patient's  blood  and not a contaminant because of the  additional testing that's required and  because of the treatment  the patients are also going to  experience an increased length