h the  pharmacy or the laboratory  with patient care so let me look at each  of these points and  provide a little bit more data to  support these comments  we'll start off with the impact of  empiric therapy  and i'm going to cite four studies and  these in fact are  four reviews looking at a large number  of studies of sepsis in patients  so as an example the first the first  study by paul  and co-workers looked at 70 studies of  sepsis in adults  and what they found was that in more  than half of these studies  greater than 25 percent of the patients  received empiric therapy that was  inappropriate  raymond and co-workers did the same type  of study but in this case they looked at  septic patients  with gra with infections caused by  gram-negative bacilli  and of the 57 studies that they looked  at  almost three-quarters of those studies  again  saw that greater than 25 of the patients  were treated  inappropriately with empiric therapy  herrera and co-workers reviewed 191  studies  of almost 75 000 patients  and they found that in one-third of the  patients  the empiric therapy was inappropriate  and finally silverberg and co-workers in  a multi-center study of 175 u.s  hospitals  observed that when inappropriate therapy  was administered  the patients with infections caused by  carbon and resistant organisms  the mortality was four-fold greater than  what you saw with  susceptible patients and that's logical  because these patients  have infections with organisms that are  resistant to almost all  antibiotics so you would expect that  you're going to see a higher proportion  of these patients  being treated inappropriately so this  underscores the value of diagnostics for  these patients  because the diagnostic tests are going  to support the detection  and diagnosis of drug resistant  infections  it's going to reduce the use of  inappropriate antibiotics because it's  going to be guiding  the therapy with the antimicrobial  susceptibility test  this results i